pjmedia: count frames at every stage of a video leg, so a black tile names its own cause

A remote tile that goes black is currently indistinguishable from a dozen different
faults. RTP statistics say packets are arriving; nothing says whether they were decoded,
and nothing at all says whether a decoded picture ever reached the renderer. Every
diagnosis therefore ends in inference, and the last three have each corrected the one
before it.

These counters end that by making the question a subtraction. Each video stream counts
frames at five points:

    captured        the camera handed a frame to the video port
    encoded         pjmedia_vid_codec_encode_begin() produced output
    decoded         pjmedia_vid_codec_decode() produced a picture
    render_submit   the frame was handed to the video device
    render_reject   the video device refused it

Read against the RTP rates already reported, the stage that stopped first is then read
off directly:

    capture 0                        camera or capture device
    capture > 0, encode 0            encoder
    encode > 0, RTP TX 0             RTP sender
    TX > 0, RX 0                     network / FreeSWITCH
    RX > 0, decode 0                 jitter buffer, decoder or keyframe
    decode > 0, render_submit 0      video port or video device
    render_submit > 0, tile black    the Android renderer, surface or GPU

`render_submit` is named for what it is. It ends at pjmedia_vid_dev_stream_put_frame(),
and on Android `andgl_stream_put_frame()` posts the frame to an OpenGL job queue and
returns -- the draw and the buffer swap happen later, on another thread. So a count here
proves pjmedia did its part and nothing more, which is exactly the boundary worth
drawing: it separates "we stopped producing" from "we produced and it was not shown".
`render_reject` is counted apart because a device that is not running returns
PJ_EINVALIDOP once per frame and is otherwise completely silent, which is the shape of a
surface that went away without pjmedia being told.

Capture and render submission happen inside pjmedia_vid_port, which has no way back to
the stream it is wired to, so pjsua -- the only party that knows the two belong together
-- hands the port the stream's counter block when it connects them, and clears it before
the stream is destroyed. That is what stops a port outliving its stream from writing into
freed memory, and what stops a rebuilt stream inheriting the counts of the one it
replaced: a new stream brings a new block, and a reused window is re-pointed rather than
accumulated onto. One caveat is recorded rather than hidden: a capture *window* can be
shared between calls, so in a mesh the last stream set up owns `captured` and the others
read zero for it; `encoded` is per stream and is the authoritative send-side count.

Cost. Each counter has exactly one writer -- the capture thread, the encoding thread, the
decoding thread, the renderer's clock -- and one reader that is allowed to be a sample
behind, so they are plain aligned 32-bit words: one add, no barrier, no allocation, no
lock on any media thread, and the same shape pjmedia_rtcp_stat already uses. Nothing is
logged per frame. The values leave the stack on the tick that already exists,
check_tx_rtcp(), at PJMEDIA_VID_STREAM_COUNTER_LOG_MSEC (5s), as one line per stream:

    vidcnt peer=192.168.2.198:26286 cap=1482 enc=1480 dec=1455 sub=1455 rej=0

The peer's RTP address is the join key, because it is what identifies a leg to the
application -- the same string it reads from StreamInfo.remoteRtpAddress -- and because
it is stable for exactly as long as the stream is. A renegotiation that moves the port
builds a new stream whose counters start at zero, which is the behaviour a rebuilt
decoder should have.

Nothing about the media changes: no codec, bitrate, resolution, keyframe request, camera
decision, stream lifecycle or conference topology is touched. This is measurement, added
so that the next change can be shown to have worked rather than asserted to have.

